# Immolate performance analysis

Date: 2026-09-02
Method: static analysis of the host (immolate.c) and kernel (search.cl, lib/*.cl,
filters/*.cl) by two independent read-only passes (one Claude Opus, one GPT-5.6),
merged and re-ranked by the coordinator. Nothing was profiled on a GPU; every
multiplier below is an estimate and is labelled as verified (read from the source)
or inferred (general GPU codegen behaviour).

Target machine reported by the user: Windows, RTX 5080 (84 SMs), 5090 available.
Observations from the user: Task Manager "3D" graph reads 100% during a run;
kernel build ("Building program...") takes roughly 5 minutes; a full sweep of the
seed pool is impractically slow.

Baseline costed against: filters/erratic_flush_five.cl. Per seed it does one
i_new, 52 randchoice_simple(R_Erratic, CARDS) draws, one 52-element bubble sort,
one 52-bin histogram, and one s_skip.

Seed pool: 2,318,107,019,761 seeds (all base-35 strings of length 0..8). At
100M seeds/s that is 6.4 hours. No single fix below makes a full sweep
practical; items 1 through 4 together are what it takes.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Ranked bottlenecks (most critical first)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1. Launch geometry starves the GPU  (immolate.c:266-267)

    size_t globalSize = numGroups * numGroups;   // 16*16 = 256 work-items
    size_t localSize  = numGroups;               // 16

Verified. Default -g 16 launches 256 work-items in 16 work-groups of 16.

Why it hurts:
- A 5080 has 84 SMs and each SM holds on the order of 1,500 resident threads,
  so the card can hold well over 100,000 work-items. 16 groups touch at most
  16 SMs.
- A work-group of 16 is half a warp (32 lanes). Every warp runs with half its
  lanes permanently masked off.
- globalSize and localSize are both derived from the single -g knob, so
  raising the thread count also changes the group shape.

Why "100% GPU" in Task Manager does not contradict this: that graph is the
fraction of time the engine had any work queued. One half-warp per SM
spinning forever reads as 100% while roughly 99% of the lanes are idle.

The user's remark that "252 occupants (84 workers * 4)" is the ceiling is
mistaken about the unit: work-items are threads, not SMs. Thousands of
work-items per SM are normal.

Cheap test: time -n 10000000 at -g 16, then -g 64, then -g 128. If throughput
climbs with G, occupancy was the wall. (Under the old geometry -g 64 means
4096 work-items in groups of 64.) If high -g values have ever hung, item 3 is
why.

Fix: decouple. localSize = the device's preferred work-group multiple (32 on
NVIDIA, typically 64 on AMD), globalSize = groups * localSize with the group
count derived from compute-unit count. Target 50k-200k work-items.
Estimated gain: 50-300x on a discrete GPU, once items 2 and 3 are fixed.
Nothing else on this list comes close.

### 2. The per-seed instance struct is ~4-5.5 KB and is fully zeroed every seed
###    (lib/instance.cl:15-40)

Verified struct contents (bool size is implementation-defined in OpenCL C,
hence the range):

    member                              1-byte bool   4-byte bool
    seed (ulong8 + int, 16-aligned)          80            80
    cache rngCache (rnginfo nodes[64])     3080          3080
    hashedSeed                                8             8
    lrandom rng                              48            48
    bool locked[ITEMS_END] (494 items)      494          1976
    instance_params params                  260           356
    total                                  3984          5552

    instance inst = {.locked = {true}};     // instance.cl:24

C99 designated-initializer semantics zero every member not named, so this
emits roughly 4 KB of stores per seed. The 64-node RNG cache (77% of the
struct) is pure waste: nextFreeNode = 0 already gates every node as
invalid, and the erratic filter allocates exactly one node. params is then
initialized a second time via a local struct and a copy (lines 29-38).

Inferred: locked[], rngCache.nodes[] and params.deckCards[] are all indexed
with runtime values, so the struct cannot be promoted to registers and lives
in NVIDIA "local" (per-thread scratch) memory. Every RNG state touch is a
memory op.

This is both a direct per-seed cost (est. 1.3-2x) and the thing that makes
item 1 affordable: 100k lanes x 5 KB is half a gigabyte of scratch traffic.

Fixes, in order of payoff:
- Initialize only the fields that are read before written. (Low risk.)
- Make CACHE_SIZE a build-time define sized to the filter; erratic needs 1
  node, a shop-heavy filter maybe 30. NOTE: too small a value silently
  overflows nodes[] in init_node, so this is not a safe default change
  without a bounds check. (Deferred.)
- Pack locked[] as a bitset (ulong[8], 64 bytes). Touches every
  inst->locked[x] site. (Deferred, mechanical but invasive.)

### 3. The result path is undefined behaviour and will hang once item 1 is fixed
###    (search.cl:7-14)

    if (score >= filter_cutoff[0]) {
        printf(...);
        if (score > filter_cutoff[0]) {
            filter_cutoff[0] = score;           // plain non-atomic store
            barrier(CLK_GLOBAL_MEM_FENCE);      // inside a divergent branch
        }
    }

Verified, three separate defects:
a. barrier() must be reached by every work-item in the group along converged
   control flow. Here it is two levels deep in a score-dependent branch. It
   is harmless today only because localSize 16 is sub-warp, where the barrier
   compiles to nothing. At 32 or more lanes some lanes skip it and the group
   deadlocks.
b. filter_cutoff[0] = score is a plain store racing every other lane's read.
   Two lanes with different scores can clobber each other (lost update).
c. filter_cutoff[0] is a non-volatile global read inside the loop; the
   compiler may hoist it, so "cutoff rises over time" is not guaranteed.

Also: kernel-side printf goes through a serialized device ring buffer. With
the default cutoff of 1 on erratic_flush_five, every seed has a max card
multiplicity of at least 1, so a large fraction of seeds print. Output may
be truncated and it dominates timing on that filter with that cutoff.

Fix chosen (user decision): drop the rising cutoff entirely. The -c value is
passed as a plain kernel argument and every seed scoring at or above it is
printed. No global write, no atomic, no barrier, no per-seed global read.
Perf gain alone is modest; this is a correctness gate for item 1. Replacing
printf with a compact result buffer drained by the host is the right
long-term shape but changes output timing semantics. (Deferred.)

### 4. The RNG pipeline is fp64-heavy on a card that runs fp64 at 1/64 rate

Three pieces, all per RNG draw; erratic does 52 draws per seed.

4a. randomseed runs LuaJIT's 10-round warmup before every draw
    (lib/util.cl:154-175). Verified call chain: every random / randint /
    randchoice with num > 0 calls randomseed(get_node_child(...)). Each draw
    is therefore 11 Tausworthe steps of 64-bit shift/xor math, roughly
    16,000 64-bit ALU ops per erratic seed; inferred ~40k scalar
    instructions since consumer GPUs have no native 64-bit shifter.
    This is semantically required: Balatro calls math.randomseed before each
    math.random and the warmup is part of LuaJIT's randomseed. The steps are
    GF(2)-linear over the state, so the 10 rounds can be collapsed into a
    precomputed jump-ahead. Est. 3-5x on the RNG share. HIGH RISK: needs
    bit-exact validation against a large seed corpus. (Deferred.)
    Low-risk partial: _randint writes lr->state[i] back to scratch four
    times per call; hoisting state into locals during randomseed removes
    ~44 scratch stores per draw. (Deferred; compiler likely does it.)

4b. roundDigits calls pow(10, 13) and does an fp64 divide on every draw
    (lib/util.cl:74-77, called from instance.cl:72). The pow almost
    certainly constant-folds after inlining but is not guaranteed to;
    OpenCL's double pow is permitted up to 16 ulp error, so a literal 1e13
    is at least as correct as the call. The divide does not fold. Replacing
    the divide with a multiply by 1e-13 is NOT bit-exact and must be
    validated before shipping. (Divide kept.)

4c. pseudohash does one serially dependent fp64 divide per character
    (lib/util.cl:41-53) and takes a 260-byte text by value; text_concat
    copies two of them per call. Per erratic seed: 8 chars in i_new plus 15
    chars for the one node = 23 serialized divides. The seed suffix is
    re-hashed for every node created; because the loop runs from the last
    character to the first, pseudohash(prefix + seed) can be computed as a
    continuation from pseudohash(seed), hashing the seed once per seed.
    Est. ~1.1x for erratic, 2-4x on the hashing share for 30-node filters.
    (Continuation deferred: needs care with the absolute (i+1) index term.)
    Passing text by pointer is mechanical. (Done.)

### 5. Erratic-deck dead work  (lib/functions.cl:588-611)

Verified. init_erratic_deck draws 52 cards then bubble-sorts them: 1,326
compares with a data-dependent swap branch that diverges across the warp, on
scratch memory. erratic_flush_five then only histograms the result, which is
order-independent. The sort matters only for filters that go on to shuffle.

The values come from CARDS[], a fixed contiguous 52-value range, so a
counting sort produces the identical array with O(52) work and no
divergence. Est. 1.2-1.8x for erratic-deck filters, nothing for others.

Every other filter checked already rejects before its expensive pack/shop
generation, and packs cannot be truncated early because duplicate locking
changes later resampling results.

### 6. The five-minute kernel build  (immolate.c:230)

The NVIDIA OpenCL front end recompiles ~4,000 lines of double-heavy kernel
on every run. Fixed cost, no throughput change, but it is paid on every
iteration while tuning. Fix: fetch the built binary once via
CL_PROGRAM_BINARIES, cache it on disk keyed on (device, driver, build
options, contents of every transitively included .cl file), and reload via
clCreateProgramWithBinary. Fall back to a source build on any failure.

### 7. Seed stepping  (lib/seed.cl:3, 83-100)

s_skip does a full base-35 divide and modulo across all 8 digits per seed.
NUM_CHARS is declared as a __constant variable rather than a literal, so
strength reduction of / and % to a multiply-high is not guaranteed
(inferred; LLVM usually does it anyway). Roughly 5-15% on a light filter if
it was not folding. A specialized small-stride add-with-carry is a further
small win. (Define done; specialization deferred.)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Verified vs inferred
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Verified from source: struct layouts, call chains, loop trip counts,
initializer semantics, launch parameters, the barrier/race defects, the
bubble sort, the filter early-exit ordering.
Inferred: cycle costs of fp64 divide/pow and 64-bit shifts, scratch-memory
placement of the instance, whether pow and NUM_CHARS constant-fold, all
speedup multipliers. Confirm the last two from a binary dump before investing
in the deferred RNG work.

