## Side note: macOS / Apple Silicon
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Does not build as-is (CL/cl.h vs OpenCL/cl.h include path; no __APPLE__
branch in getExecutableDir). With those two host fixes it compiles and lists
the GPU, but every filter fails at clCreateKernel with "newComputePipelineState
failed" because Apple's OpenCL-on-Metal has no fp64 and the kernel is
double throughout. Apple's OpenCL front end does accept the source, so a Mac
is usable as a syntax checker for kernel edits, not as a runner.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Changes applied (2026-09-02) — low-risk set
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
All 29 filters compile against Apple's OpenCL front end after these edits (used
purely as a syntax/type check; Apple cannot run the fp64 kernel). Nothing was
executed on an NVIDIA GPU from this machine, so the golden-output diff described
above still needs to be run on the Windows box before trusting results.

Host (immolate.c, lib/immolate.h, CMakeLists.txt)
- Launch geometry decoupled. localSize is now the kernel's preferred
  work-group multiple from the device (rounded up to 64 where allowed);
  -g is the number of work-groups and defaults to 8 per compute unit when
  omitted (84 CUs -> 672 groups x 32 = 21,504 work-items on a 5080).
  First Windows measurements (RTX 5080, erratic_flush_five, 20M seeds,
  -c 11): -g 84 -> 1.42 s, -g 336 -> 0.76 s, versus ~45 s on the old build,
  i.e. 30-60x. An initial version rounded localSize up to 64, which the
  driver rejected with CL_INVALID_WORK_GROUP_SIZE (-54) for this kernel;
  the launch now uses the preferred multiple as-is and halves it once on
  that error.
  The old meaning of -g (G*G work-items in groups of G) is gone; -g 1 -n 1
  still works for analyzer runs. Docs updated.
- Compiled-kernel binary cache. After a source build the device binary is
  saved to .kernel_cache/<hash>.bin next to the executable, where the hash
  covers the build options, device name/vendor/version, driver version, the
  generated program text (which carries the filter name) and the full
  contents of the filter file and every lib/*.cl file. Later runs load it
  with clCreateProgramWithBinary. Any load or build failure prints a notice
  and falls back to a normal source build. Because some drivers (Apple's,
  observed here) accept garbage bytes at clCreateProgramWithBinary and only
  fail at clCreateKernel, a cache-loaded program that cannot produce the
  kernel also deletes the cache file and rebuilds from source once.
  --no_cache bypasses it. .kernel_cache/ is gitignored.
  This removes the ~5 minute rebuild on every run.
- CL_TARGET_OPENCL_VERSION raised 100 -> 120 in CMakeLists.txt. Required:
  CL_KERNEL_PREFERRED_WORK_GROUP_SIZE_MULTIPLE is an OpenCL 1.1 query and
  the headers hide it under a 1.0 target.
- macOS builds now (OpenCL/cl.h include, _NSGetExecutablePath). Runtime on
  Apple Silicon still fails at kernel creation for lack of fp64.

Kernel
- search.cl: the cutoff is now a plain kernel argument (the -c value) and
  every seed scoring at or above it is printed. The old behaviour raised the
  cutoff to the best score seen so far through a non-atomic global write plus
  a barrier() inside a divergent branch, which is undefined behaviour and
  would deadlock at the new work-group sizes. An intermediate version used
  atom_max; the user chose the simpler fixed-cutoff semantics instead, so
  there is no global write, no atomic and no per-seed global read at all.
  FIXED_FILTER_CUTOFF is now a no-op (documented in docs/DOCUMENTATION.md).
  Note that with erratic_flush_five the default -c 1 matches every seed, so
  pass a meaningful -c for that filter.
- lib/instance.cl i_new: no aggregate initializer, so the 64-node RNG cache
  (~3 KB) is no longer zeroed per seed. Fields that were read before
  written (locked[], params.*, rng, cache counters) are initialized
  explicitly to the same values as before. Cache nodes are only read at
  indices below nextFreeNode and init_node writes them first, so leaving
  them uninitialized is safe.
- lib/instance.cl get_node_child and lib/cache.cl resample_str: node
  strings are built with an in-place text_append(text*, const text*)
  instead of the by-value text_concat that copied three 260-byte structs
  per call. pseudohash takes const text*.
- lib/util.cl roundDigits: 10^13 is a literal instead of pow(10, 13) for
  the d == 13 case every caller uses. 1e13 is exactly representable so a
  correct pow returns the same value; OpenCL permits pow up to 16 ulp of
  error, so the literal can only be more faithful, never less. The divide
  is unchanged (a reciprocal multiply is not bit-exact).
- lib/functions.cl init_erratic_deck: the 52 draws happen in the same
  order, but each card is tallied into a 52-bin count as it is drawn and
  the sorted deck is written out from the counts. The result is identical
  to bubble-sorting (all values are the contiguous enum range C_2..S_T),
  in 104 non-divergent steps instead of 1,326 divergent compares.
  sort_deck itself is left in place for any other caller.
- lib/seed.cl: NUM_CHARS is #define 35 instead of a __constant variable so
  the / and % in s_skip and s_next are guaranteed to strength-reduce.

Expected effect
- Geometry: measured 30-60x on a 5080 (see above). The remaining question
  is how far group count scales; the register footprint of the instance
  struct is what limits resident groups per SM, so the deferred
  CACHE_SIZE / bitset work is the next lever on occupancy.
- Everything else: roughly 1.5-3x combined on erratic_flush_five from the
  removed zeroing, sort, struct copies and pow; less on other filters.
- Build cache: minus ~5 minutes per run after the first.

Validate on Windows with the golden-output procedure above. Because the
default cutoff of 1 prints a large fraction of seeds on erratic_flush_five,
use -c 3 or higher for the comparison so output volume does not dominate.
